"settings.help.selectionPopupTitle" = "Auto-Popup on Text Selection";
"settings.help.selectionPopupBody" = "Like PopClip: select text anywhere and a small bar with the local quick actions appears right next to it automatically — no hotkey needed.\n\nEnable: Settings → General → \"Auto-popup on text selection\". Off by default, since otherwise it would appear for every text selection system-wide.\n\nPosition is configurable: below, above, right, or left of the selection — if another app (Safari's \"Look Up\", Pages' formatting bar, …) already shows its own popup in the same spot, just move Tippi's out of the way.\n\nClicking an icon applies the action immediately and replaces the selection — the exact same actions as the hotkey popup (uppercase/lowercase, underscore, convert umlauts, brackets, character/word count, …).\n\nSeparated by a divider at the end: the translate icon — opens the Translate Quick Panel with the selection pre-filled instead of replacing it directly.\n\nThe bar closes by itself when you run an action, click elsewhere, press Escape, switch to another app, move the pointer well away from the selection and the bar, or leave it unused for five seconds. For that same selection it only comes back when you select the text again — a click next to it does not bring it back.";
"settings.help.customWordsTitle" = "Custom words";
"settings.help.customWordsBody" = "Under “Dictionary & Snippets → Custom words” you add terms transcription keeps getting wrong — brands, product and proper names, jargon. Tippi enforces your spelling wherever the term appears.\n\nImportant: the list never inserts words that were not spoken, and never alters other words to resemble your entries. It only corrects the spelling and capitalisation of the terms you listed.\n\nWhy it is needed: models hear the name correctly but normalise its spelling to whatever looks like an ordinary word — CINEWEB becomes CineWeb. No model can guess a house spelling, it has to be told.\n\nThe list takes effect during AI cleanup of a dictation. Weaker models do not always obey it: measured on 2026-09-15, Gemma 4 E2B and Qwen 3.5 4B honoured it reliably, Qwen 3.5 2B did not.";
"settings.help.espansoImportTitle" = "Importing Espanso snippets";
"settings.help.espansoImportBody" = "Tippi lists the Espanso match files in your folder as import candidates. They only run once you take them over with “Import” — the snippets then live in Tippi's own store, survive a moved file, and no longer need Espanso.

A file sitting in the folder is therefore not enough. Up to version 2.9 Tippi read approved files straight from disk; that path was removed because the approval could be forged. Coming from 2.9, import your files once.

Snippets without shell commands are active immediately. One with a shell command asks once — per snippet, showing the exact command. That approval is cryptographically bound to this command and re-checked before every expansion. If the command changes, the approval is void and Tippi asks again.

Two files may define the same trigger. Only one of them takes effect; the other is marked “Overridden” in the list, and every row names its source file. Delete one of the two entries to decide which applies.";
"settings.help.localModelsTitle" = "Which local model for dictation?";
"settings.help.localModelsBody" = "The MLX models offered in Settings were measured on 2026-09-15 against real German dictations, using Tippi's own cleanup prompt and the same path the app uses in production.\n\n• Gemma 4 E2B (3.6 GB) — 0 findings, 0.52 s. Recommended, and the default.\n• Qwen 3.5 4B (3.1 GB) — 0 findings, 0.89 s.\n• Qwen 3.5 2B (1.7 GB) — smallest download, but 6 findings: left filler words in, kept abandoned clauses, dropped sentence punctuation and ignored the custom-word list. Fine for notes, wrong for anything you send.\n• Qwen 3.5 9B (6.0 GB) — largest model, not measured.\n\nScoring covered filler removal, German noun capitalisation, commas, self-corrections, brand spelling, and whether the model cleaned the text instead of replying to it.\n\nTippi does not download these models itself — the local MLX server fetches them from HuggingFace on first use. Progress is shown in Settings, the download happens once and is cached afterwards.";
"settings.help.snippetsTitle" = "Text Snippets";
"settings.help.snippetsBody" = "Type a shortcut anywhere — Tippi replaces it instantly with the stored text. Like Espanso, but built in.\n\nEnable: Snippets tab → \"Enable text snippets\". Needs Accessibility permission (System Settings → Privacy & Security, then enable Tippi in the accessibility list (renamed on macOS 26+)).\n\nCreate your own snippet — click \"New snippet\", enter a trigger and replacement text, done. Example: \":mlg\" → \"Best regards\\nMichael\".\n\nDynamic snippet (with the current date) — in the editor, click \"Insert Variable\", pick e.g. \"Weekday this week\" → Thursday → format \"DD. Month\". Tippi inserts a placeholder automatically and recalculates the date live every single time — never a frozen date.\n\nExample – the \":nl\" cinema-week snippet: combines three variables into one text — this week's Thursday, Thursday + 6 days, current year. Result: \"Cinema week from Sep 10 to Sep 16, 2026\", automatically current every week, without you ever touching it again.\n\nImporting Espanso files — existing Espanso match files (~/Library/Application Support/espanso/match/) are detected and shown automatically (read-only, the file stays the source of truth). The first time a file loads, Tippi asks for one-time approval, showing exactly what the file contains before anything goes live.\n\n⚠️ If an already-approved file's content changes later, Tippi asks again — a silently altered file can never hijack an existing trigger unnoticed.";
"settings.help.syncTitle" = "What travels to my other Macs?";
"settings.help.syncBody" = "Signed in to several Macs with the same Apple ID, Tippi carries your custom words and custom prompts across automatically. There is nothing to set up.\n\nDeliberately left behind:\n\n• API keys. They live in the Keychain and stay there — Apple syncs the Keychain itself, encrypted. The settings store Tippi uses is not built for secrets.\n• Approvals for snippets that run a shell command. The key signing them is bound to this device. If it travelled, approving a command on one Mac would authorise it everywhere. A synced shell snippet shows \"Approval no longer verifiable\" on the second Mac and asks once — by design.\n• The local model choice and the MLX port. Both depend on that Mac's hardware.\n• Anything holding a file path, such as the watched snippet folder.\n\nChange the same value on two Macs and the newer change wins. Snippets themselves do not travel yet; that needs a merge that loses nothing even when both Macs added something at the same time.";
"settings.help.emojiTitle" = "Inserting emoji";
"settings.help.emojiBody" = "Two ways, depending on whether you already know which emoji you want.\n\n1) Picker (⌥⌘E): Opens a Spotlight-style window. Typing filters, arrow keys select, Return inserts — the emoji lands wherever your cursor was. Recently used ones appear first. The hotkey is configurable under Hotkeys.\n\n2) Shortcodes while typing: Type :rocket: and it becomes 🚀 instantly, anywhere on your Mac, no hotkey needed. Found under Snippets → Emoji.\n\nGerman and English both work: :rakete: as well as :rocket:, and :kino: finds 🍿. Plus 135 hand-picked shortcuts for everyday words — :thumbsup: 👍, :heart: ❤️, :thanks: 🙏, :check: ✅, :idea: 💡.\n\n3) Emoticons: Common text emoticons convert as you type — :-) ;-) :( :D <3 XD and around 25 more. Separate toggle under Snippets → Emoji; when off, the : picker and search remain exactly as they are. Only fires after a space or at the start of a line, so code like a[:(b)] or http:// is left alone.\n\nSuggestion list: As soon as you type :e, a list of matching emoji appears next to your cursor. Space accepts the top one, a click picks any, typing more narrows it, Escape dismisses it. Arrow keys and Return belong to the app you are writing in and deliberately do nothing here — use ⌥⌘E for keyboard browsing. Can be switched off under Snippets → Emoji.\n\nIf Tippi does not know a name, your text is left exactly as typed — it never guesses. A shortcode must contain at least one letter, so timestamps (12:30:) and score lines (10:1:) can never turn into an emoji by accident. Your own snippets take precedence if a trigger collides.\n\nSkin-tone variants are deliberately excluded — they would multiply every person emoji by six and flood search results.";
"settings.help.importExportTitle" = "Share & import prompts";
"settings.help.importExportBody" = "Export your custom prompts as a .tippipack file to share them with teammates or keep a backup.\n\nExport all — click \"Export All\" at the top of the Prompts tab.\nExport one — click the ↑ icon next to any prompt.\nImport — click \"Import\", select a .tippipack file, then choose Merge (keeps your existing prompts) or Replace (replaces them).\n\nImported prompts always get fresh IDs, so they never conflict with existing ones.";
"settings.help.apiTitle" = "AI providers & keys";
"settings.help.apiBody" = "Add your API key in the Providers tab. Tippi supports 11 providers — OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Mistral, Scaleway (EU, Paris), Groq, Kimi/Moonshot, Nebius (EU, Amsterdam) and OpenRouter (300+ models behind one key) as cloud BYOK providers, plus Ollama and MLX (both fully local, free, no key needed). Keys are stored securely in the macOS Keychain.\n\nDefault provider: Tippi tries your chosen default first; if it has no key it falls through to the next configured one. With no explicit default it automatically uses the first provider you've added a key for, so a fresh install never stalls on a cold local model. Optional fallback (Providers tab): if your provider fails (rate limit, server or network error) Tippi can retry the next configured provider — off by default since it sends your text to a second provider.";

"settings.help.linksTitle" = "Links & resources";
"settings.help.linksBody" = "[GitHub repository](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi) — public, MIT-licensed, source code, issues, releases.\n\n[Landing page](https://miwixyz.github.io/Tippi/) — bilingual EN/DE, features, screenshots, download.\n\n[One-pager](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/blob/main/docs/ONE-PAGER.md) — for Hacker News, Reddit, sharing with friends; what Tippi is in 60 seconds.\n\n[Changelog](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) — every change, every version.\n\n[Latest release](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/releases/latest) — DMG download, release notes, checksums.";

