"settings.help.pickModelTitle" = "Which model for what? — recommendations";
"settings.help.pickModelBody" = "There is no best model, only fitting ones. Three rules of thumb:\n\n1) Short tasks local, long text in the cloud.\nA small local model (MLX Qwen 3.5 2B, the default) polishes dictation in ~0.6 s and is perfectly adequate for spelling, shortening and bullet points. On a long, fact-dense text — a multi-paragraph business email with names, prices and links — a 2B model hits its ceiling and may hand the text back unchanged. That's exactly what the per-prompt provider override (Settings → Prompts) and the \"Switch provider\" button on the result are for.\n\n2) Not all German is equal.\nMistral (Paris) and Claude are noticeably more accurate on German prose than comparably priced alternatives — Mistral was trained with European languages as a first-class target, not as translation garnish. For German correspondence those are the first two addresses. Nebius and Scaleway also host in the EU (Amsterdam/Paris) and are the pick when data residency matters more than the last nuance.\n\n3) Reasoning models are usually the wrong choice here.\nModels with an internal thinking phase (the gpt-5 family, Qwen3.x \"thinking\") produce a long chain of thought before any text appears. For \"fix this sentence\" that costs seconds with no quality gain. The model picker marks the fastest non-reasoning option with ⭐ — when in doubt, take that one.\n\nA concrete starting point: dictation polish on Groq (Llama 3.1 8B Instant, sub-second) or locally on MLX. Text rewriting and email replies on Claude Haiku 4.5 or Mistral Small. Anything confidential on MLX or Ollama — then not a single character leaves your Mac.";
"settings.help.mlxTitle" = "MLX — local AI on Apple Silicon";
"settings.help.mlxBody" = "MLX runs AI models directly on your Mac — no cloud, no API key, no data leaves your device. It's roughly 1.5–2× faster than Ollama on Apple Silicon.

One-time setup: pick MLX in Settings → Providers and click \"Install MLX…\". Tippi handles the rest — no Terminal needed.

The default is Gemma 4 E2B. Measured against real German dictations it was both the most accurate and the fastest; which four models are offered and what they were judged on is under \"Which local model for dictation?\". Tippi caps local response length to avoid slow runaway generations and shows the actual generation time in the preview badge.

When MLX is your default provider, Tippi pre-warms the server on app launch so your first transformation is faster.\n\nIs “Start server” stuck? Usually a server process left behind by an earlier crash is holding the port: it accepts connections but never answers. Since v2.11.7 Tippi clears such a stale server itself and then checks that the port is really free. If a foreign program holds it, nothing is terminated — the message names the program and pid so you can decide. Alternatively, change the port in this section.";
